    <section>
        <h2>Gravitational force near the surface of the Earth</h2>
        <p>Let {`\\(M\\)`} be the mass of Earth and {`\\(m\\)`} be the mass of a block near the surface of the Earth. If {`\\(R\\)`} is the radius of the Earth and {`\\(h\\)`} is the height of the block above the surface, then the gravitational force between them is given by {`\\[
            \\begin{eqnarray}
            F &=& \\frac{GMm}{(R+h)^2}\\\\
              &\\approx &\\frac{GMm}{R^2}\\\\
              &= & mg
            \\end{eqnarray}
            \\]`} 
        where {`\\(g\\)`} is called the <em>acceleration due to gravity</em> and is approximately equal to {`\\(9.8~ms^{-2}\\)`}.
        </p>
